/* ============================================================
   SECTION 3: Sampling & Simulation
   ============================================================ */
+ '<h2 class="l-title">3. Sampling & Simulation</h2>'

+ '<p class="l-text">Random numbers are not just for generating data \u2014 they power some of the most important computational techniques in science and engineering. <strong>Bootstrap sampling</strong> estimates uncertainty without parametric assumptions. <strong>Monte Carlo methods</strong> solve intractable problems through random sampling. <strong>Random walks</strong> model everything from stock prices to molecular diffusion.</p>'

+ '<p class="l-text"><strong>Monte Carlo estimator \u2014 approximate any expected value by averaging samples:</strong></p>'
+ '<div class="katex-block">$$\\hat{\\theta} = \\frac{1}{N}\\sum_{i=1}^{N} f(x_i), \\qquad \\text{error} \\sim \\mathcal{O}\\!\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{N}}\\right)$$</div>'
+ '<div class="calc-cards">'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">\u03b8\u0302</div><div class="card-body">Monte Carlo estimate \u2014 our numerical approximation of the true expected value E[f(X)].</div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">f(x_i)</div><div class="card-body">Function evaluated at each random sample x_i drawn from the target distribution. Could be an indicator function (for probabilities) or any computable quantity.</div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">N</div><div class="card-body">Number of random samples drawn. More samples = more accurate estimate.</div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">O(1/\u221aN)</div><div class="card-body">Convergence rate \u2014 to halve the error you need 4\u00d7 the samples. Slow but dimension-independent, which is why Monte Carlo wins in high dimensions.</div></div>'
+ '<div class="calc-card"><div class="card-title">Use cases</div><div class="card-body">Estimating \u03c0, evaluating integrals, simulating physical systems, Bayesian inference (MCMC), reinforcement learning rollouts.</div></div>'
+ '</div>'

+ '<div class="calc-example"><div class="example-label">BOOTSTRAP SAMPLING</div><div class="example-body"><strong>Problem:</strong> You have 100 data points and want a confidence interval for the mean, but you don\u2019t know the population distribution.<br><br><strong>Solution:</strong> Resample <em>with replacement</em> from your data thousands of times. Each resample gives a different mean. The distribution of these means gives you the confidence interval \u2014 no assumptions needed.</div></div>'

+ '<div class="code-wrap"><div class="code-label"><span>PYTHON</span><button class="copy-btn" onclick="navigator.clipboard.writeText(this.closest(\'.code-wrap\').querySelector(\'code\').textContent)">COPY</button></div><pre class="code-block"><code><span class="kw">import</span> numpy <span class="kw">as</span> np\n\nrng = np.random.<span class="fn">default_rng</span>(<span class="num">42</span>)\n\n<span class="cm"># Original data: 100 exam scores</span>\nscores = rng.<span class="fn">normal</span>(loc=<span class="num">72</span>, scale=<span class="num">12</span>, size=<span class="num">100</span>)\n<span class="fn">print</span>(f<span class="str">"Sample mean: {scores.mean():.2f}"</span>)\n\n<span class="cm"># Bootstrap: resample 10,000 times</span>\nn_bootstrap = <span class="num">10000</span>\nboot_means = np.<span class="fn">empty</span>(n_bootstrap)\n<span class="kw">for</span> i <span class="kw">in</span> <span class="fn">range</span>(n_bootstrap):\n    sample = rng.<span class="fn">choice</span>(scores, size=<span class="fn">len</span>(scores), replace=<span class="kw">True</span>)\n    boot_means[i] = sample.<span class="fn">mean</span>()\n\n<span class="cm"># 95% confidence interval</span>\nci_low = np.<span class="fn">percentile</span>(boot_means, <span class="num">2.5</span>)\nci_high = np.<span class="fn">percentile</span>(boot_means, <span class="num">97.5</span>)\n<span class="fn">print</span>(f<span class="str">"95% CI: [{ci_low:.2f}, {ci_high:.2f}]"</span>)</code></pre></div>'

+ '<div class="calc-example"><div class="example-label">MONTE CARLO: ESTIMATING PI</div><div class="example-body"><strong>Idea:</strong> Throw darts at a unit square. The ratio of darts landing inside the inscribed quarter-circle to total darts approximates \u03c0/4. With enough darts, we get a precise estimate of \u03c0.</div></div>'

+ '<div class="code-wrap"><div class="code-label"><span>PYTHON</span><button class="copy-btn" onclick="navigator.clipboard.writeText(this.closest(\'.code-wrap\').querySelector(\'code\').textContent)">COPY</button></div><pre class="code-block"><code><span class="kw">import</span> numpy <span class="kw">as</span> np\n\nrng = np.random.<span class="fn">default_rng</span>(<span class="num">42</span>)\n\n<span class="cm"># Monte Carlo estimation of pi</span>\nn_points = <span class="num">1000000</span>\nx = rng.<span class="fn">random</span>(n_points)   <span class="cm"># x in [0, 1)</span>\ny = rng.<span class="fn">random</span>(n_points)   <span class="cm"># y in [0, 1)</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># Check if inside quarter circle: x\u00b2 + y\u00b2 \u2264 1</span>\ninside = (x**<span class="num">2</span> + y**<span class="num">2</span>) <= <span class="num">1.0</span>\npi_estimate = <span class="num">4.0</span> * inside.<span class="fn">sum</span>() / n_points\n<span class="fn">print</span>(f<span class="str">"Estimated \u03c0: {pi_estimate:.6f}"</span>)   <span class="cm"># ~3.14159</span>\n<span class="fn">print</span>(f<span class="str">"True \u03c0:      {np.pi:.6f}"</span>)</code></pre></div>'

+ '<p class="l-text"><strong>Random walk simulation</strong> models stochastic processes. At each time step, the walker takes a random step. This simple model describes stock prices (geometric Brownian motion), molecular diffusion, and even PageRank.</p>'

+ '<div class="code-wrap"><div class="code-label"><span>PYTHON</span><button class="copy-btn" onclick="navigator.clipboard.writeText(this.closest(\'.code-wrap\').querySelector(\'code\').textContent)">COPY</button></div><pre class="code-block"><code><span class="kw">import</span> numpy <span class="kw">as</span> np\n\nrng = np.random.<span class="fn">default_rng</span>(<span class="num">42</span>)\n\n<span class="cm"># Simulate 5 random walks, each 200 steps</span>\nn_walks = <span class="num">5</span>\nn_steps = <span class="num">200</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># Each step is +1 or -1 with equal probability</span>\nsteps = rng.<span class="fn">choice</span>([-<span class="num">1</span>, <span class="num">1</span>], size=(n_walks, n_steps))\n\n<span class="cm"># Cumulative sum gives position at each time</span>\npositions = np.<span class="fn">cumsum</span>(steps, axis=<span class="num">1</span>)\n<span class="cm"># positions.shape = (5, 200)</span>\n\n<span class="cm"># Statistics</span>\n<span class="fn">print</span>(f<span class="str">"Final positions: {positions[:, -1]}"</span>)\n<span class="fn">print</span>(f<span class="str">"Max displacement: {np.abs(positions).max()}"</span>)\n<span class="fn">print</span>(f<span class="str">"RMS displacement at step 200: {np.sqrt((positions[:,-1]**2).mean()):.2f}"</span>)</code></pre></div>'

/* --- Plotly: Random Walk (EN) --- */
+ '<div id="plot-walk-en" class="plotly-graph"></div>'
