+ 'Plotly.newPlot("plot-loss-en",[t],layout,{responsive:true,displayModeBar:false});'
+ '},200)</script>'

+ '<div class="calc-graph"><div class="graph-caption"><strong>What this graph shows:</strong> The cross-entropy loss decreasing over 200 epochs of gradient descent. The loss starts at ln(2) \u2248 0.693 (random chance for binary classification) and converges to a low value. The decreasing curve confirms that our pure-NumPy gradient descent is correctly optimizing the logistic regression model.</div></div>'

+ '<div class="think-box"><strong>Think about it:</strong> This entire ML pipeline \u2014 data generation, preprocessing, feature engineering, training, and evaluation \u2014 used only NumPy. Libraries like scikit-learn and PyTorch build on these exact same operations. Understanding the NumPy layer means you truly understand what happens when you call <code>model.fit()</code>.</div>'

+ '<div class="calc-example"><div class="example-label">NUMPY MASTERY CHECKLIST</div><div class="example-body"><strong>After completing this course, you should be able to:</strong><br><br>1. Create, reshape, and slice arrays fluently (Lessons 1\u20132)<br>2. Use broadcasting and vectorization to eliminate Python loops (Lesson 3)<br>3. Apply statistical functions and linear algebra operations (Lessons 4\u20135)<br>4. Generate reproducible random data and run simulations (Lesson 6)<br>5. Save/load arrays efficiently in binary and text formats (Lesson 6)<br>6. Build a complete ML pipeline from scratch with pure NumPy (Lesson 6)<br><br>You are now ready for <strong>Pandas</strong> (structured data) and <strong>scikit-learn</strong> (production ML).</div></div>'

+ '<div class="l-warn"><strong>Course Complete!</strong> You\u2019ve finished all 6 lessons of the NumPy course. Next recommended course: <strong>Pandas</strong> \u2014 where you\u2019ll learn to work with labeled, heterogeneous data in DataFrames, building directly on the NumPy foundation you\u2019ve mastered here.</div>'

